# Comparison of All Three Solutions

| Solution             | Main Idea                                                                                | Time                                            | Space                     |
| -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- | ------------------------- |
| **Solution 1**       | Simulate assigning letters to the 8 keys using a dictionary                              | **O(n)**                                        | **O(1)** (at most 8 keys) |
| **Solution 2**       | Build a frequency array, sort it, then assign costs by groups of 8                       | **O(n)** (sorting 26 elements is constant time) | **O(1)**                  |
| **Optimal Solution** | Realize only the length of the word matters; compute costs directly using `(i // 8) + 1` | **O(n)**                                        | **O(1)**                  |

## Key Learning Pattern

When you can **reorder or remap elements freely**, ask yourself:

1. **Does the actual value matter, or only the rank/order?**
2. **Can I group positions by their cost?**
3. **Can the entire mapping be replaced by a simple mathematical formula?**

In this problem, once you realize that every letter is distinct and the mapping is completely flexible, the characters themselves become irrelevant. The answer depends only on how many letters occupy the 1-push, 2-push, 3-push, and 4-push groups, leading to the elegant formula:

[
\text{cost of the } i^\text{th} \text{ assigned letter} = \left(\left\lfloor \frac{i}{8} \right\rfloor + 1\right)
]

This observation reduces the solution to a single loop with constant extra space.
